      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=-2+4t \\\\ z=3+t\\end{aligned}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,1,0⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,4,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨0,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4 \\\\ 4y+z & =4 \\\\ 4y+z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
